window.CALCQ=[{"id":"cb:c1-transfer-local","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity I","unit":"Continuity I","q":"Suppose $\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=5$ but $f(2)=1$. Which conclusion is justified?","o":["The limit is $1$","The limit is $5$, but $f$ is not continuous at $2$","The limit does not exist","The function is continuous because both sides agree"],"a":1,"hint":"Check the two-sided limit first, then compare it with the actual value.","expl":"The two one-sided limits agree at 5, so the limit exists. It differs from f(2)=1, so continuity fails.","fb":["The isolated value does not determine nearby behavior.","","Unequal function value does not destroy an existing limit.","Agreement of the sides gives a limit, but continuity also requires agreement with the value."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c2-transfer-definition","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity II","unit":"Continuity II","q":"For $f(x)=x^2$, which limit computes $f'(3)$ by definition?","o":["$\\lim_{h\\to0}[(3+h)^2-9]/h$","$\\lim_{h\\to0}[(3+h)^2-3]/h$","$\\lim_{h\\to0}(3+h)^2/h$","$\\lim_{h\\to0}(h^2-9)/h$"],"a":0,"hint":"Subtract the output at the target from the output at the nearby input.","expl":"The definition is limh→0​[f(3+h)−f(3)]/h. Simplifying for h=0 gives 6+h, so the derivative is 6.","fb":["","Subtract f(3)=9, not the input three.","The original output must be subtracted.","The nearby input is 3+h, not just h."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-cancel","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"For $f(x)=x^2$, the difference quotient is $\\frac{2xh+h^2}{h}$ for $h\\ne0$. What is the correct next step?","o":["Set $h=0$ immediately","Cancel only one $h$ and keep $2xh+h$","Conclude the derivative is undefined","Factor $h$, cancel it, then take $h\\to0$"],"a":3,"hint":"Every term in the numerator contains a common factor h.","expl":"For h=0, the quotient is 2x+h. Its limit is 2x; the original quotient need not be defined at h=0.","fb":["Substitution into the unsimplified quotient produces 0/0, not a derivative.","Factor the entire numerator; each term is divided by h.","The quotient can be undefined at zero while having a finite limit there.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-definition","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"For $f(x)=x^2$, what should you write first when using the $h$-definition?","o":["$f(x+h)=(x+h)^2$","$f(x+h)=x^2+h$","$f(x+h)=2x+h$"],"a":0,"hint":"Replace the entire input x by x+h before expanding.","expl":"The original rule squares its input, so f(x+h)=(x+h)2.","fb":["","Adding h to the output is not the same as replacing the input.","This is the simplified difference quotient, not the shifted function."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-domain","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"The definition gives $f'(x)=-2/x^3$ for $f(x)=1/x^2$. Where does this derivative apply?","o":["All real $x$","Only $x>0$","Only $x<0$","All real $x\\ne0$"],"a":3,"hint":"A derivative can only be taken at an input where the original function is defined in the required neighborhood.","expl":"The original function and its derivative are defined for every real x=0, but not at zero.","fb":["The original function is not defined at zero.","Negative nonzero inputs also belong to the original function domain.","Positive inputs also belong to the original function domain.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-rate","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"If $s(t)$ is position, what does $s'(5)$ represent?","o":["Position at $t=5$","Instantaneous velocity at $t=5$","Average velocity from $0$ to $5$"],"a":1,"hint":"Position has units of distance; its time derivative has units of distance per time.","expl":"s′(5) is the limit of average velocities over intervals shrinking toward time 5.","fb":["Position is s(5), without the derivative mark.","","Average velocity requires a difference of positions divided by a nonzero interval."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-secant","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"As the second point on a secant approaches the point of tangency, what happens to $\\Delta x$?","o":["$\\Delta x=1$","$\\Delta x\\to\\infty$","$\\Delta x\\to0$"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of the horizontal distance between the two approaching inputs.","expl":"The horizontal change approaches zero; when the finite slope limit exists, it gives the tangent slope.","fb":["A fixed gap of one does not make the two inputs approach each other.","An increasing gap moves the inputs farther apart, not closer.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-slope","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"Which expression gives the average rate of change of $f$ from $x_1$ to $x_2$, where $x_1\\ne x_2$?","o":["$f(x_2)-f(x_1)$","$\\frac{f(x_2)-f(x_1)}{x_2-x_1}$","$\\frac{x_2-x_1}{f(x_2)-f(x_1)}$"],"a":1,"hint":"Identify the change in output and divide by the nonzero change in input.","expl":"Slope is Δy/Δx, so it is the output difference divided by the input difference.","fb":["This gives only the output change; divide by the input interval too.","","This reverses rise and run; it measures the reciprocal rate instead."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c21-guided-tangent","src":"calc","topic":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","unit":"Limits & continuity · 2.1","q":"For $f(x)=x^2+x-3$, which is the tangent line at $x=2$?","o":["$y=5x$","$y-5=3(x-2)$","$y-3=5(x-2)$","$y=3$"],"a":2,"hint":"Find both f(2) and f′(2); one supplies the point, the other the slope.","expl":"f(2)=3 and f′(2)=5, so point-slope form is y−3=5(x−2).","fb":["The tangent must pass through (2,3), not automatically through the origin.","This reverses the function value and derivative: the slope is five.","","A horizontal line has slope zero, but the derivative here is five."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-higher","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"If $s(t)$ is position, what does $s''(t)$ represent?","o":["Acceleration","Velocity","Position"],"a":0,"hint":"Follow position to its first time derivative, then differentiate once more.","expl":"Velocity is s′(t) and acceleration is s′′(t).","fb":["","Velocity is the first derivative, not the second.","Position is the original function, before either derivative."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-power","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"Differentiate $f(x)=7x^5$.","o":["$7x^4$","$35x^4$","$35x^5$"],"a":1,"hint":"Multiply by the old exponent, then reduce that exponent by one.","expl":"f′(x)=7(5x4)=35x4.","fb":["The old exponent five also multiplies the coefficient.","","The exponent decreases from five to four after differentiation."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-product","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"Which setup is correct for $y=x^2\\sin x$?","o":["$y'=2x\\cos x$","$y'=2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x$","$y'=x^2\\cos x$"],"a":1,"hint":"Differentiate one factor at a time while keeping the other factor unchanged.","expl":"The Product Rule gives both contributions: 2xsinx+x2cosx.","fb":["Multiplying the two derivatives is not the Product Rule.","","This omits the term obtained by differentiating x squared."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-quotient","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"For $y=\\frac{x-1}{x+1}$, which numerator appears after the Quotient Rule?","o":["$(x-1)-(x+1)$","$1$","$(x+1)-(x-1)$"],"a":2,"hint":"Keep denominator times derivative of numerator first, then subtract the other product.","expl":"The numerator is (x+1)(1)−(x−1)(1)=2, and the denominator is (x+1)2.","fb":["Reversing the subtraction changes the derivative sign.","The derivatives of numerator and denominator are both one, but their difference is not the required numerator.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-rewrite","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"Which is the simplest rewrite-first approach to differentiating $1/x^3$?","o":["Differentiate numerator and denominator separately","Use the Quotient Rule without simplifying","Rewrite it as $x^{-3}$"],"a":2,"hint":"A reciprocal power can be written with a negative exponent.","expl":"For x=0, write x−3, then use the Power Rule. The Quotient Rule is valid but unnecessarily longer here.","fb":["A quotient derivative is not the quotient of the derivatives.","The Quotient Rule can work, but the requested simplest approach uses a negative power.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-speed","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"At one instant, $v=-6$ m/s and $a=2$ m/s$^2$. What is the speed?","o":["$-6$ m/s","$2$ m/s","$6$ m/s","$-12$ m/s"],"a":2,"hint":"Speed is the magnitude of velocity, not acceleration.","expl":"speed=∣v∣=∣−6∣=6 m/s. The sign of velocity describes direction.","fb":["Speed cannot be negative; take the absolute value.","Two is the acceleration, with different units.","","There is no multiplication of velocity by acceleration in the speed definition."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-guided-trig","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"Find $\\frac{d}{dx}(4\\sin x-3\\cos x)$, with angles in radians.","o":["$4\\cos x+3\\sin x$","$4\\cos x-3\\sin x$","$4\\sin x+3\\cos x$"],"a":0,"hint":"The derivative of cosine contributes a negative sign.","expl":"Using (sinx)′=cosx and (cosx)′=−sinx gives 4cosx+3sinx.","fb":["","The cosine derivative is negative; the original minus sign reverses it.","The sine term must become cosine, and the cosine term must become negative sine."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-predict-motion","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"For $s(t)=-0.81t^2+2$, what happens after differentiating the velocity once more?","o":["Keep $-1.62t$","Return to $-0.81t^2+2$","Obtain the constant acceleration $-1.62$"],"a":2,"hint":"First velocity is linear in time; its derivative is its constant slope.","expl":"v(t)=−1.62t and a(t)=−1.62.","fb":["The time factor disappears when the linear velocity is differentiated.","Differentiating again does not undo the first derivative.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-predict-product","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"For $y=x^2\\sin x$, which pair identifies the two factors?","o":["$x^2$ and $\\sin x$","$2x$ and $\\cos x$","$x^2$ and $\\cos x$"],"a":0,"hint":"Label the original factors before taking their derivatives.","expl":"The displayed product is x squared times sine x. Its factors are not their derivatives.","fb":["","These are their derivatives, not the original factors.","The second factor in the original expression is sine, not cosine."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-predict-quotient","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"For $g(x)=(x-1)/(x+1)$, what denominator must the unsimplified Quotient Rule derivative have?","o":["$x+1$","$(x+1)^2$","$(x-1)^2$"],"a":1,"hint":"The Quotient Rule squares the original denominator.","expl":"The denominator is (x+1)2, with x=−1.","fb":["The Quotient Rule requires the square, not the first power.","","This squares the numerator instead of the denominator."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-predict-rewrite","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"For $4/x^3+6\\sqrt{x}$, where is the real derivative formula valid?","o":["$x\\ge0$","All real $x$","$x>0$"],"a":2,"hint":"Combine the real square-root restriction with the nonzero denominator.","expl":"The real square root requires x≥0 and the denominator excludes zero; hence x>0.","fb":["Zero is excluded by the denominator x cubed.","Negative inputs do not give a real square root.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:c223-predict-terms","src":"calc","topic":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","unit":"Derivative rules · 2.2–2.3","q":"For $f(x)=5x^4-3x^2+7x-9$, what does the constant term contribute to $f'(x)$?","o":["$-9$","$0$","$-9x$"],"a":1,"hint":"A constant does not change as the input changes.","expl":"The derivative of the constant term is zero.","fb":["The unchanged constant is not its derivative.","","Differentiation does not turn a constant into a linear term."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:chainChecks-identify","src":"calc","topic":"Chain rule","unit":"Chain rule","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Name the complete expression receiving the outermost operation.","expl":"Work from the outside inward. The expression being raised to the fifth power is the inside function.","fb":["This describes the outside function after naming its input u.","","This is part of a derivative, not the original inside function."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:chainChecks-mixed","src":"calc","topic":"Chain rule","unit":"Chain rule","q":"For $y=x^2\\sin(3x)$, which derivative uses both required rules?","o":["$6x\\cos(3x)$","$2x\\cos(3x)$","$x^2\\cos(3x)$","$2x\\sin(3x)+3x^2\\cos(3x)$"],"a":3,"hint":"Apply Product Rule to the whole expression and Chain Rule inside the sine factor.","expl":"The product contributes two terms: 2xsin(3x) and x2[3cos(3x)].","fb":["Multiplying the derivatives omits the two product-rule terms.","Differentiating both factors at once is not Product Rule.","This omits the derivative of x2 and the inner factor 3.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:chainChecks-order","src":"calc","topic":"Chain rule","unit":"Chain rule","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Differentiate each layer and multiply their derivatives.","expl":"Differentiate the outside while keeping the inside, then multiply by the derivative of the inside.","fb":["Composition is not addition; the outside derivative must be evaluated at g(x).","The inside factor must be its derivative, and the outside must receive g(x).",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:chainChecks-power","src":"calc","topic":"Chain rule","unit":"Chain rule","q":"For $y=(2x^3+1)^4$, which factor is lost in the incorrect derivative $4(2x^3+1)^3$?","o":["$4$","$6x^2$","$2x^3+1$","$3$"],"a":1,"hint":"Differentiate the whole inside polynomial separately.","expl":"The inside derivative is 6x2, so y′=24x2(2x3+1)3.","fb":["The outside factor 4 is already present.","","This is the inside itself, which is already kept inside the power.","The exponent falls to three, but this is not the inner derivative."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:chainChecks-trig","src":"calc","topic":"Chain rule","unit":"Chain rule","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Sine changes to cosine; the inner derivative supplies another factor.","expl":"Outside: sine becomes cosine. Keep 4x, then multiply by its derivative 4.","fb":["","The derivative of sine is cosine, not sine.","You differentiated the outside but lost the inner derivative 4."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-bisection","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"After evaluating a midpoint in the bisection method, which half-interval should be kept?","o":["Always the left half","Always the right half","The half whose endpoint values have opposite signs","The half with the larger function values"],"a":2,"hint":"First check whether the midpoint itself is a zero; otherwise retain opposite signs.","expl":"Keep the half where the endpoint function values have opposite signs.","fb":["The sign test, not left versus right, decides the retained half.","The sign test, not left versus right, decides the retained half.","","Larger outputs do not identify which half contains the sign change."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-closed","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"To be continuous on $[a,b]$, what is required at $a$?","o":["$\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f(x)=f(a)$","$\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)=f(a)$","$f(a)=0$","No endpoint condition"],"a":1,"hint":"At the left endpoint, approach through points inside the interval.","expl":"At the left endpoint use right-hand continuity.","fb":["The left side lies outside the interval at its left endpoint.","","The endpoint value can be any finite number; it must match the appropriate limit.","Both endpoint values must agree with their interior one-sided limits."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-discontinuity","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"If $\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)$ are finite but unequal, what type of discontinuity occurs at $x=a$?","o":["Removable","Jump","Continuous","A hole only"],"a":1,"hint":"Compare the two finite one-sided limits.","expl":"Unequal finite one-sided limits produce a jump discontinuity.","fb":["A removable discontinuity requires equal finite one-sided limits.","","Continuity requires the one-sided limits to agree.","A hole alone has a shared nearby destination, unlike a jump."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-families","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"Where can $f(x)=\\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-9}$ fail to be continuous?","o":["Only at $x=0$","$x=\\pm3$","Everywhere","Nowhere"],"a":1,"hint":"Solve the denominator equation before deciding the domain.","expl":"A rational function is continuous on its domain; exclude the zeros of the denominator.","fb":["At zero the denominator is negative nine, not zero.","","Away from denominator zeros, a rational function is continuous.","Check the denominator: it has two real zeros."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-ivt","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"If $f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and $K$ lies between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$, IVT guarantees:","o":["Exactly one $c$","$f(c)=K$ for at least one $c\\in[a,b]$","$f(c)=0$ always","$f$ is linear"],"a":1,"hint":"Separate the hypotheses from the existence conclusion.","expl":"IVT guarantees at least one such value; it does not guarantee uniqueness.","fb":["IVT proves existence, not uniqueness.","","The target is K, which need not be zero.","IVT applies to continuous nonlinear functions too."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-piecewise","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"At a breaking point $x=c$ of a piecewise function, what should you compare?","o":["Only $f(c)$","Left limit, right limit, and $f(c)$","Only the slopes","Only the domain"],"a":1,"hint":"Evaluate each rule on its own side of the break.","expl":"The two one-sided limits must agree, and their common value must equal f(c).","fb":["The value alone does not test nearby behavior.","","Equal slopes are not the definition of continuity.","The domain alone does not show whether the pieces meet."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-point","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"For $f$ to be continuous at $x=c$, which statement must be true?","o":["Only $f(c)$ exists","Only the limit exists","$f(c)$ exists, the limit exists, and $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)$","The graph must be a line"],"a":2,"hint":"Test the value, the two-sided limit, and whether they agree.","expl":"Continuity at a point requires all three conditions.","fb":["A defined value alone says nothing about nearby behavior.","A limit may exist while the point is missing or misplaced.","","Curved graphs can be continuous; being a line is not required."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-predict-0","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"What value repairs the missing point at $x=1$?","o":["$0$","$1$","$2$","No value can repair it"],"a":2,"hint":"Cancel the common factor only for nearby inputs.","expl":"The nearby rule is x+1, whose limit at 1 is 2.","fb":["Zero is not the nearby output: simplify to x+1 for x not equal to one.","One is the input target, not the approached output.","","The two-sided limit is finite, so defining the missing point to equal it repairs continuity."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-predict-1","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"Which continuity equation determines $m$?","o":["$6+m=8$","$m=8$","$6=m$","$6+m=9$"],"a":0,"hint":"Substitute the breaking input into each rule.","expl":"The left limit is 6+m and the right limit and value are 8.","fb":["","The left rule contributes six as well as m; do not drop that term.","Equate the whole left expression to the right limit, not one term to the parameter.","The right rule is x squared minus one, so its output at three is eight, not nine."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-predict-2","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"At $x=3$, what is the common limit and value of $|2x-6|$?","o":["$3$","$6$","DNE","$0$"],"a":3,"hint":"Evaluate both pieces at their shared boundary.","expl":"Both pieces approach 0, which equals the function value.","fb":["Three is the breaking input, not the output.","Substitute three into the whole expression: six minus six is zero.","Both one-sided limits exist and agree because both linear pieces approach zero.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-predict-3","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"Is $x\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ continuous at $0$?","o":["No, because floor always forces a jump","Yes: both limits and the value are $0$","Only from the left","Only from the right"],"a":1,"hint":"Near zero, the product is −x on the left and 0 on the right.","expl":"Both one-sided limits are 0, and the value at zero is 0.","fb":["The factor x tends to zero and removes the jump in this product at zero.","","The right-hand limit also equals zero; continuity holds from both sides.","The left-hand rule is minus x, whose limit is also zero."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-predict-4","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"What does IVT alone prove for this polynomial on $[0,1]$?","o":["Exactly one zero","The zero equals $1/2$","At least one zero in $(0,1)$","No zero"],"a":2,"hint":"Check continuity and the signs of the endpoint outputs.","expl":"The polynomial is continuous and its endpoint values straddle zero, so at least one zero lies inside.","fb":["Existence does not imply uniqueness; that needs a separate argument.","IVT does not locate the zero exactly; the polynomial at one half is negative seven eighths.","","The endpoint signs differ and the polynomial is continuous, so IVT guarantees a zero."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-predict-5","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"After the first midpoint test, which interval retains the sign change?","o":["$[1,1.5]$","$[1.5,2]$","$[1,1.25]$","$[0,1]$"],"a":0,"hint":"Compare the negative value at one with the positive value at one and a half.","expl":"The signs at 1 and 1.5 are opposite, so this is the next bracket.","fb":["","Both endpoint outputs on this half are positive, so it loses the sign-change bracket.","The first midpoint is one and a half; one and a quarter is a later midpoint.","Zero to one is not a half of the current interval one to two."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:continuity-special","src":"calc","topic":"Continuity","unit":"Continuity","q":"For $f(x)=\\lfloor x\\rfloor$, compare the one-sided limits at an integer $n$.","o":["Both equal $n$","$\\lim_{x\\to n^-}f(x)=n-1$ and $\\lim_{x\\to n^+}f(x)=n$","Both equal $n-1$","Both do not exist"],"a":1,"hint":"Just below an integer, the floor is the preceding integer.","expl":"The greatest integer function jumps at every integer.","fb":["Immediately below n, the floor is n minus one.","","Immediately above n, the floor is n.","Each one-sided limit exists; it is the two-sided limit that fails."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-candidates","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"For $(x+2)/(x^2-9)$, which excluded values remain vertical asymptotes?","o":["$3$ only","$-3$ only","$-3$ and $3$","Neither"],"a":2,"hint":"Factor x2−9 and evaluate the numerator at each zero.","expl":"At 3 the numerator is 5; at −3 it is −1. Neither cancels, so both are VAs.","fb":["Also inspect the other factor x+3; its numerator is nonzero.","Also inspect x-3; its numerator is nonzero.","","The numerator x+2 cancels neither x-3 nor x+3."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-check-factor","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Check the reduced denominator at the excluded value.","expl":"A cancelled denominator factor indicates a possible hole. A factor that remains in the denominator can create a vertical asymptote.","fb":["","A completely removed denominator zero need not create unbounded behavior.","The original expression is still undefined at its excluded input.","A horizontal asymptote concerns behavior far away, not an excluded finite input."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-check-graph","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Compare the two sides; do not average infinity.","expl":"Opposite unbounded one-sided behavior gives a vertical asymptote; infinity is not a real-number limit.","fb":["Opposite infinities cannot be averaged to zero.","Unbounded outputs do not approach any real number.","","Continuity requires a finite function value and matching limit."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-check-power","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Test an even power at small positive and negative inputs.","expl":"An even power has the same positive sign on both sides (except at the zero itself).","fb":["An odd power changes sign; an even positive power does not.","","An even power of a negative number is positive too.","The power is zero only at c, not throughout a neighborhood."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-check-trig","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Rewrite tangent as sine divided by cosine.","expl":"Rewrite tanx=sinx/cosx and inspect zeros of cosx.","fb":["Sine is the numerator for tangent, not its denominator.","","Tangent is not a rational function with denominator x.","The denominator in the sine/cosine representation is cosine."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-classify","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"For $(x^2-4x+3)/(x^2-x-6)$, classify $3$ and $-2$.","o":["VA at both","Hole at both","VA at 3; hole at -2","Hole at 3; VA at -2"],"a":3,"hint":"Factor as (x−1)(x−3)/[(x−3)(x+2)].","expl":"The factor x−3 cancels completely, leaving a finite limit at 3. At −2, the remaining numerator is −3 and denominator approaches zero.","fb":["The common factor x-3 cancels completely; inspect the reduced expression.","The factor x+2 does not cancel, so the limit at -2 is unbounded.","These classifications are reversed: x-3 cancels, x+2 remains.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-cot","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"Where are all vertical asymptotes of $\\cot(2x)$?","o":["$x=n\\pi/2,\\ n\\in\\mathbb Z$","$x=\\pi/4+n\\pi/2,\\ n\\in\\mathbb Z$","$x=n\\pi,\\ n\\in\\mathbb Z$ only","None"],"a":0,"hint":"Use cot(2x)=cos(2x)/sin(2x).","expl":"sin(2x)=0 when 2x=nπ, and the numerator is (−1)n=0. Hence every nπ/2 is a VA.","fb":["","Those are cosine zeros, which give zeros of cotangent rather than VAs.","This misses odd multiples of pi/2; solve for x after setting 2x=n pi.","Sine has zeros where the cosine numerator remains nonzero."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-direction","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"For $-2/(x-4)$, predict the left and right limits at $4$, respectively.","o":["$-\\infty,+\\infty$","$+\\infty,-\\infty$","$+\\infty,+\\infty$","$0,0$"],"a":1,"hint":"Check the sign of the denominator separately on each side.","expl":"Left: negative divided by a small negative is positive unbounded; right: negative divided by a small positive is negative unbounded.","fb":["You reversed the signs: the numerator is negative, not positive.","","The denominator changes sign across 4, so the branches have opposite directions.","A nonzero numerator divided by a quantity approaching zero does not approach zero."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-property","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"Predict $\\lim_{x\\to(\\pi/2)^-}5\\tan x$.","o":["$-\\infty$","$0$","$+\\infty$","$5$"],"a":2,"hint":"From the left, sinx→1 and cosx→0+.","expl":"Tangent tends to +∞ from the left. Multiplication by positive 5 preserves the direction.","fb":["Cosine approaches zero positively from this side, so tangent is positive.","A numerator near 1 divided by a tiny positive denominator is unbounded.","","The constant does not replace tangent; it multiplies its unbounded behavior."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-15-sketch","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","unit":"Limits: graphical & numeric (L1.5)","q":"Both one-sided limits at $-1$ are $+\\infty$. What must the nearby sketch show?","o":["Both branches fall","Left rises; right falls","The point (-1,0)","Both branches rise toward x=-1"],"a":3,"hint":"Separate input direction (left/right) from output direction (up/down).","expl":"The asymptote is x=-1 and each nearby branch rises without bound. These limits do not specify the value at -1.","fb":["Positive infinity means rising, not falling.","The plus sign is the output direction on both sides.","Limits describe nearby behavior and do not specify a point on the asymptote.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-abs","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"What is the first algebraic step for $|3x+6|$?","o":["Differentiate","Solve $3x+6=0$","Square the expression","Set x=0 only"],"a":1,"hint":"Locate where the expression inside the absolute value changes sign.","expl":"The zero of the inside expression is the breaking point.","fb":["Differentiation is unnecessary; the sign of the inside determines the rewrite.","","Squaring would change the function instead of identifying its two rules.","Zero is not necessarily the breaking input; solve for the inside to vanish."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-equation","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"Does $3x-y=5$ define y as a function of x?","o":["Only when x>0","Yes, $y=3x-5$","No, because x and y are both present","No, because it is linear"],"a":1,"hint":"Isolate y and count its outputs for a chosen x.","expl":"Solving for y gives one value for each x.","fb":["The isolated linear rule accepts every real x, not just positive inputs.","","Having both variables in an equation does not prevent it defining a function.","A nonvertical line is a function of x."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-family","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"What is the first feature to read from $y=|x-4|+1$?","o":["Vertex $(4,1)$","Slope 4","x-intercept 1","A circle center"],"a":0,"hint":"Read the horizontal and vertical shifts in absolute-value vertex form.","expl":"Absolute-value vertex form reveals the vertex immediately.","fb":["","The number four is a horizontal shift, not a slope.","The outside one gives the vertex height, not an x-intercept.","An absolute-value graph is V-shaped, not circular."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-floor1","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"What is $\\lfloor-0.7\\rfloor$?","o":["$0$","$1$","$-0.7$","$-1$"],"a":3,"hint":"Choose the greatest integer not exceeding negative seven tenths.","expl":"The greatest integer less than or equal to -0.7 is -1.","fb":["Zero is greater than the input; floor is not truncation toward zero.","Positive one is greater than the input.","The floor output must be an integer.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-grapheq","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"A V-graph has vertex $(-2,3)$. Which skeleton is correct?","o":["$y=a|x+2|+3$","$y=a|x-2|+3$","$y=a|x+3|-2$","$y=ax^2+2$"],"a":0,"hint":"Use y=a∣x−h∣+k and substitute the vertex coordinates.","expl":"Vertex form uses x-h, so h=-2 gives x+2.","fb":["","This puts the vertex at positive two instead of negative two.","The horizontal and vertical coordinates have been swapped.","A quadratic skeleton gives a parabola rather than a V."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-piece","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"At $x=3$, which condition owns the point: $0<x\\le3$ or $x>3$?","o":["Both","Neither","$0<x\\le3$","$x>3$"],"a":2,"hint":"Equality decides which interval contains the boundary.","expl":"The condition 0<x≤3 includes 3 because of the equality; x>3 excludes it.","fb":["The two conditions cannot both contain three because one is strict.","The condition with the equality does contain three.","","The strict inequality excludes three."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-power1","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"Which statement is true for $y=x^7$?","o":["It is y-axis symmetric","Both ends rise","It is a step function","It has odd-power behavior"],"a":3,"hint":"Test (−x)7 and compare with x7.","expl":"7 is odd, so the graph has the general odd-power shape.","fb":["An odd power changes sign, so it has origin rather than y-axis symmetry.","For this odd power, negative inputs give negative outputs and positive inputs positive outputs.","A positive-integer power is continuous, not a staircase.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-22-vlt","src":"calc","topic":"Function families (L2.2)","unit":"Function families (L2.2)","q":"A vertical line crosses a graph at two points. What can you conclude?","o":["It must be linear","It has no domain","The graph is not y as a function of x","It is always a function"],"a":2,"hint":"The vertical line fixes one input and tests how many outputs it has.","expl":"One x-value would have two different y-values.","fb":["The test does not determine whether the graph is linear.","The relation has x-values; its failure is uniqueness of output.","","Two distinct crossings assign two outputs to one input."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-23-change","src":"calc","topic":"Rates of change (L2.3)","unit":"Rates of change (L2.3)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Net change is final output minus initial output.","expl":"Net change is f(4)−f(1)=−2−3=−5, a decrease of five output units.","fb":["Adding outputs does not give net change.","Subtract the actual endpoint outputs: negative two minus three.","","This reverses the subtraction or takes the absolute value."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-23-compare","src":"calc","topic":"Rates of change (L2.3)","unit":"Rates of change (L2.3)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"An equation in x is solved by the horizontal coordinate of a crossing.","expl":"Graphical equations are solved using x-coordinates of intersection points.","fb":["Subtracting coordinates does not produce the intersection input.","","Five is the shared output, not the input solving the equation.","Adding the coordinates has no role in solving the intersection equation."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-23-end","src":"calc","topic":"Rates of change (L2.3)","unit":"Rates of change (L2.3)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"Parentheses exclude an endpoint; brackets include it.","expl":"An open endpoint is excluded; a filled endpoint is included.","fb":["The left endpoint is open, so negative two must be excluded.","The right endpoint is filled, so four must be included.","Both endpoint choices are reversed.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-23-first","src":"calc","topic":"Rates of change (L2.3)","unit":"Rates of change (L2.3)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Domain records which inputs are represented.","expl":"Domain is the collection of all permitted x-coordinates.","fb":["","The vertical axis gives output values and therefore range.","Domain uses input coordinates only; the other axis gives range.","Every graph point has an input coordinate to project."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-23-inc","src":"calc","topic":"Rates of change (L2.3)","unit":"Rates of change (L2.3)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Follow the curve as input increases, regardless of the sign of the output.","expl":"A downward trend as x increases means decreasing.","fb":["","Increasing means rising as x increases, the opposite of the description.","A constant function would stay at the same height.","A decreasing curve still has defined outputs on the interval."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-23-max","src":"calc","topic":"Rates of change (L2.3)","unit":"Rates of change (L2.3)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"Distinguish the input location, the point, and the output value.","expl":"The value is the output, y=4; the point is (-1,4).","fb":["Negative one is where the maximum occurs, not its value.","The ordered pair is the maximum point; its value is just the output.","Adding the coordinates does not define an extreme value.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-26-even","src":"calc","topic":"Transformations (L2.6)","unit":"Transformations (L2.6)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Compute f(−x) and prove equality with f(x) or −f(x).","expl":"f(−x)=(−x)5+(−x)=−x5−x=−f(x) for every real x, so the function is odd.","fb":["A function on a symmetric domain that is both even and odd must be identically zero; this one is not.","","Both terms change sign, so f(−x)=−f(x) rather than f(x).","Both terms have odd powers, and their sum satisfies the odd identity."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-26-map","src":"calc","topic":"Transformations (L2.6)","unit":"Transformations (L2.6)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"Solve 2(X−4)=1 for the new input, then transform the output.","expl":"x becomes 4+1/2=4.5; y becomes 3·2−1=5.","fb":["Divide the old input by two before adding four; do not multiply it by two.","The outside rule is 3y−1, so the new output is five.","The new input solves X=4+1/2, not four minus one.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-26-rule","src":"calc","topic":"Transformations (L2.6)","unit":"Transformations (L2.6)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"For a left shift, the inside input reaches each old value sooner.","expl":"Inside +2 gives a horizontal shift left 2.","fb":["Outside plus two shifts vertically up.","An outside factor stretches the outputs instead of shifting horizontally.","","Inside minus two shifts the graph right."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-26-shift","src":"calc","topic":"Transformations (L2.6)","unit":"Transformations (L2.6)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Inside operations act inversely on the input; outside operations act directly on the output.","expl":"Inside +4 moves left 4, and outside −1 moves down 1.","fb":["","Inside plus four moves the point left, not right.","Outside minus one moves the output down, not up.","Both the horizontal and vertical directions are reversed."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-a-g2","src":"calc","topic":"Function toolkit","unit":"Function toolkit","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Follow the curve toward x=2.","expl":"The curve approaches the open point at (2,4).","fb":["The filled point gives the function value. Trace the curve toward the open point for the limit. Follow the curve toward x=2.","","The vertical axis is a scale, not the destination. Trace both branches toward the target and read their shared height.","The title does not determine a limit. Follow the curve toward x=2 from both sides."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-compound","src":"calc","topic":"Compound inequalities","unit":"Compound inequalities","q":"What is the preferred first move for a compound fraction?","o":["Simplify the top only","Flip the whole fraction","Cross multiply","Find one LCD for all small denominators"],"a":3,"hint":"One common multiplier must clear every small denominator in both large parts.","expl":"One LCD clears all small fractions efficiently.","fb":["Simplifying the numerator alone can be valid, but leaves the denominator fractions unresolved; one LCD clears both large parts efficiently.","Taking the reciprocal changes the value unless that operation is explicitly required.","This is one expression, not an equation with two ratios to cross multiply.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-d1","src":"calc","topic":"Function toolkit","unit":"Function toolkit","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Focus on nearby inputs.","expl":"A limit describes nearby outputs.","fb":["A limit describes nearby outputs; it does not require f(4) to exist or equal 7.","","The notation says the input approaches 4. It is the nearby outputs, not the inputs, that approach 7.","A limit is a statement about nearby behavior, not a point the graph must cross. Here inputs approach 4 and outputs approach 7."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-d2","src":"calc","topic":"Function toolkit","unit":"Function toolkit","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Bracket the target.","expl":"The inputs approach from both sides.","fb":["The input exactly at 2 does not show nearby behavior. Choose values close to 2 from both sides.","These inputs move away from 2 and do not bracket it. Choose closer inputs on both sides.","","Values above 2 show only the right-hand behavior. Include nearby inputs below 2 as well."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-d4","src":"calc","topic":"Function toolkit","unit":"Function toolkit","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Approach from larger inputs.","expl":"The right branch is at 4.","fb":["1 is the target input. Read the height of the branch for inputs greater than 1.","The minus sign means inputs less than the target; the plus sign means inputs greater than it. Approach from larger inputs.","","The two-sided limit fails, but the right-hand limit exists: follow only inputs greater than 1."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-d5","src":"calc","topic":"Function toolkit","unit":"Function toolkit","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Use the filled point.","expl":"The filled point at (2,1) gives f(2)=1; the open point is not included.","fb":["","You used the input or the point at the target. A limit follows nearby outputs instead. Use the filled point.","An open point is not included. Use the filled point to find the function value. Use the filled point.","An open point is not included. Use the filled point to find the function value. Use the filled point."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-division","src":"calc","topic":"Polynomial division","unit":"Polynomial division","q":"What comes immediately after changing division to multiplication?","o":["Expand first","Use the reciprocal, then factor and cancel","Add denominators","Cross multiply"],"a":1,"hint":"Division by a nonzero expression means multiplication by its reciprocal.","expl":"Division becomes multiplication by the reciprocal before simplification.","fb":["Expansion usually hides factors that could cancel; use the reciprocal first.","","Adding denominators is not a rule for division.","Cross multiplication is an equation-solving operation, not the division rule here."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-dne-jump","src":"calc","topic":"Limits that fail to exist","unit":"Limits that fail to exist","q":"If $\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f(x)=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)=5$, what is $\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)$?","o":["$2$","$3.5$","$5$","DNE"],"a":3,"hint":"A two-sided limit must agree with both one-sided limits. Compare 2 and 5; do not average them.","expl":"Unequal one-sided limits are never averaged. The two-sided limit does not exist.","fb":["That uses only the left side. The right side approaches 5, so the two sides disagree.","A limit is not an average of the two sides. Neither side approaches 3.5 here.","That uses only the right side. The left side approaches 2, so the two sides disagree.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-factor-completely","src":"calc","topic":"Factoring","unit":"Factoring","q":"What should you ask next? $$4x(x^2-16)$$","o":["$\\text{Can I cancel }x?$","$\\text{Can I factor again?}$","$\\text{Can I add terms?}$"],"a":1,"hint":"Inspect the remaining parenthesis: are both terms squares with subtraction between them?","expl":"x2−16 is still a difference of squares.","fb":["There is no denominator here, so there is no factor to cancel against.","","The two terms inside are not like terms and cannot be added together."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-gcf-check","src":"calc","topic":"Factoring","unit":"Factoring","q":"What is the FIRST step? $$18x^4y^2-24x^3y^5$$","o":["$\\text{Use grouping}$","$\\text{Use difference of squares}$","$\\text{Find the GCF}$"],"a":2,"hint":"Look for the largest shared integer factor and the smallest exponent of each shared variable.","expl":"Always check the GCF first.","fb":["There are only two terms here; first remove their shared factor.","A shared factor is present and must be removed before considering special products.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-graphical","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: graphical","unit":"Limits: graphical","q":"On the hole graph above, what is $\\lim_{x\\to2}f(x)$?","o":["$1$","$2$","$4$","DNE"],"a":2,"hint":"Cover the filled point. Trace the nearby curve toward x=2 from both sides and read the height.","expl":"Trace the curve from both sides. Both branches approach the open point at height 4.","fb":["1 is the height of the filled point: it gives f(2). For the limit, trace the nearby curve instead.","2 is the target on the horizontal axis. Read the height approached on the vertical axis.","","A hole alone does not prevent a limit. Check whether the branches approach the same height."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-identity-method","src":"calc","topic":"Trigonometric identities","unit":"Trigonometric identities","q":"What is a valid way to prove an identity?","o":["Transform one side algebraically until it becomes the other side.","Assume both sides are equal and stop.","Check only one numerical value."],"a":0,"hint":"An identity must hold for every allowed input, not just a selected example.","expl":"An identity proof shows an algebraic transformation.","fb":["","Assuming the conclusion gives no proof.","One numerical check cannot establish an identity for all inputs."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-inequality-absolute","src":"calc","topic":"Rational inequalities","unit":"Rational inequalities","q":"Solve $|2x-3|\\le5$.","o":["$[-1,4]$","$x\\le-1\\ \\text{or}\\ x\\ge4$","$(-1,4)$","$[-4,1]$"],"a":0,"hint":"A distance at most five gives an AND inequality with both endpoints included.","expl":"−5≤2x−3≤5 gives −2≤2x≤8, hence −1≤x≤4.","fb":["","This outside region corresponds to a greater-than type inequality.","Equality is allowed, so both endpoints are included.","Adding three before dividing gives boundaries negative one and four."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-inequality-negative","src":"calc","topic":"Rational inequalities","unit":"Rational inequalities","q":"Solve $-3x+2\\le11$.","o":["$x\\le-3$","$x\\ge-3$","$x\\ge3$","$x\\le3$"],"a":1,"hint":"Subtract two first, then divide by a negative number.","expl":"−3x≤9 gives x≥−3 because division by −3 reverses the sign.","fb":["The inequality must reverse when dividing by a negative number.","","Nine divided by negative three is negative three.","Both the boundary sign and inequality direction are incorrect."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-inequality-rational","src":"calc","topic":"Rational inequalities","unit":"Rational inequalities","q":"Which solution solves $\\frac{x-1}{x+2}\\ge0$?","o":["$(-\\infty,-2)\\cup[1,\\infty)$","$(-\\infty,-2]\\cup[1,\\infty)$","$[-2,1]$","$(-2,1]$"],"a":0,"hint":"Split the number line at the numerator zero and the denominator zero, then test signs.","expl":"The quotient is positive for x<−2 or x>1, equals zero at 1, and is undefined at −2.","fb":["","Negative two makes the denominator zero and can never be included.","The denominator zero cannot be included, and the middle interval has negative sign.","The quotient is negative between the two critical numbers."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-inequality-repeated","src":"calc","topic":"Rational inequalities","unit":"Rational inequalities","q":"For $(x-4)(x+2)^2<0$, which solution set correctly treats the repeated zero?","o":["$(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(-2,4)$","$(-2,4]$","$(4,\\infty)$","$(-\\infty,4)$"],"a":0,"hint":"The squared factor is positive away from its zero; strict inequality excludes every zero.","expl":"Away from −2, the sign is the sign of x−4. Thus x<4, but both zeros −2 and 4 are excluded.","fb":["","Values below negative two also work, and four must be excluded.","Above four, both factors are positive.","At negative two the product is zero, not strictly negative."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-lcd","src":"calc","topic":"Rational expressions","unit":"Rational expressions","q":"What determines the LCD?","o":["All required factors from the factored denominators","The largest numerator","Only common factors","The first denominator only"],"a":0,"hint":"Factor each denominator and keep every distinct factor with its greatest required power.","expl":"Include each denominator factor to the greatest power needed.","fb":["","Numerators do not determine a common denominator.","Common factors alone can miss factors needed by one denominator.","The first denominator may not contain factors required by the other fractions."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-m1","src":"calc","topic":"Function toolkit","unit":"Function toolkit","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Trace the line toward x=2.","expl":"Both sides approach 5.","fb":["You used the input or the point at the target. A limit follows nearby outputs instead. Trace the line toward x=2.","Simplify for nearby inputs, then calculate again. The target input itself can remain excluded. Trace the line toward x=2.","","A two-sided limit needs evidence from inputs below and above the target. Trace the line toward x=2."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-neg-exp","src":"calc","topic":"Negative exponents","unit":"Negative exponents","q":"Rewrite using positive exponents. $$x^{-5/3}$$","o":["$x^{3/5}$","$\\frac{1}{x^{5/3}}$","$-x^{5/3}$"],"a":1,"hint":"A negative exponent means reciprocal, not a negative coefficient; the base must be nonzero.","expl":"A negative exponent means reciprocal; it does not make the expression negative.","fb":["Taking a reciprocal does not invert the exponent fraction.","","A minus sign on the exponent does not negate the value."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-numerical","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: numeric","unit":"Limits: numeric","q":"A table near $x=3$ shows outputs $5.9,5.99,6.01,6.1$ as inputs close in from both sides. What is the best estimate of $\\lim_{x\\to3}f(x)$?","o":["$3$","$6$","$9$","DNE"],"a":1,"hint":"Compare 5.9,5.99 with 6.1,6.01. Which output do both groups get closer to?","expl":"Values from both sides are approaching 6. A table gives evidence for an estimate, not proof of all nearby behavior.","fb":["3 is the input target, not the approached output. Follow the numbers in the output column toward a shared value.","","Do not add the input target to the output. Compare the outputs just below and just above 6.","An output need not equal the limit exactly. These outputs get closer to one shared number from both sides."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-one-sided","src":"calc","topic":"One-sided limits","unit":"One-sided limits","q":"On the jump graph, what is $\\lim_{x\\to1^-}f(x)$?","o":["$1$","$2$","$4$","DNE"],"a":1,"hint":"The minus sign means inputs less than 1. Follow only the branch to the left of the target.","expl":"The minus sign means approach from inputs less than 1. The left branch approaches 2.","fb":["1 is the target input. Read the output height of the branch to its left.","","4 is the right-hand height. The minus sign asks you to use inputs less than 1.","The two-sided limit fails here, but this question asks for only the left-hand limit. That branch has a definite height."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-center","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Match (x−h)2+(y−k)2=r2.","expl":"Reverse the signs for the center and take the square root of 36.","fb":["The right side is r2=36, not r=36.","The expression x+5 means x−(−5), so the horizontal center is negative five.","","Reverse the signs inside both parentheses, and take the square root for the radius."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-diameter","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"The center is halfway between the two endpoints.","expl":"The midpoint identifies the center.","fb":["","Slope gives a direction, not the center.","Circumference requires the radius, which has not been found yet.","An intercept does not determine the center of a general circle."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-distance","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Use the horizontal and vertical changes as perpendicular legs.","expl":"The changes are 3 and 4, so the distance is 5.","fb":["","Adding the coordinate changes gives a path along two legs, not the straight-line distance.","Square each change before adding inside the square root.","The sum of squares is the squared distance; take its square root."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-graph","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Use the new center, but keep the original squared radius.","expl":"The center signs appear oppositely inside the parentheses; the right side is r².","fb":["This equation leaves the center at the origin.","","These signs move the center to (−3,2) instead.","The vertical center sign is wrong and the radius must be squared."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-midpoint","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"Average each coordinate separately.","expl":"Average the corresponding coordinates.","fb":["You added coordinates but did not divide each sum by two.","These are half the coordinate differences, not the averages.","The vertical sum is −3+5=2, so its average is positive one.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-normalize","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Make both squared coefficients equal to one using an equivalent equation.","expl":"Normalize the squared coefficients before completing the square.","fb":["Normalize first to use the monic completing-square routine correctly.","Moving one linear term does not normalize the squared coefficients.","","Dividing only some terms changes the equation and the circle."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-square","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"Complete one square in x and another in y.","expl":"Keep the x-work and y-work separated before completing both squares.","fb":["The expression is already expanded; separate the two variables before making squares.","Midpoint belongs to coordinate pairs, not this algebraic conversion.","A square root cannot separate this sum into two completed squares.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-tangent","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Distance to the horizontal axis uses the vertical coordinate.","expl":"Distance to the x-axis is the absolute value of the y-coordinate.","fb":["","Two is the distance to the y-axis, not the x-axis.","Do not add horizontal and vertical distances; only perpendicular distance matters.","Subtracting the coordinate magnitudes does not give distance to an axis."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc19-v2-write","src":"calc","topic":"Circles (PC19)","unit":"Circles (PC19)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Substitute the center into the standard form and square the radius.","expl":"Use opposite signs inside the parentheses and square the radius.","fb":["The horizontal center is positive one, requiring x−1.","","These signs describe the opposite center (−1,3).","Both the vertical center sign and the squared-radius value need correction."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-common-mistake","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"A function restricts outputs per input, not inputs per output.","expl":"Different inputs may share the same output. A relation fails to be a function when one input is assigned more than one output.","fb":["Unique outputs are required for an inverse function to be a function, not for every function itself.","Inputs matter: each one must still have exactly one assigned output.","","Several inputs may share the same output."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-domain-rule","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"A real even root allows a zero radicand unless another restriction excludes it.","expl":"An even root is defined when its radicand is nonnegative.","fb":["Strict positivity is needed for an even root in a denominator, not from the numerator root alone.","A denominator must be nonzero, not equal to zero.","A negative radicand has no real even root.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-dq","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Build both outputs before subtracting the whole second expression.","expl":"Find f(a+h) and f(a) separately before subtracting. Parentheses help prevent sign errors.","fb":["","Domain restrictions matter but do not construct the numerator.","Range does not supply the two outputs needed in the numerator.","A graph is optional; the algebraic quotient uses two substitutions."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-eval","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Replace x by the entire input (−a) before squaring.","expl":"Replace every x with the complete input (-a) before simplifying.","fb":["This drops both the negative input and the square.","The substitution must retain the square on the whole input.","","The square applies to −a together; it does not make the coefficient negative."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-function","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"For each permitted input, count how many outputs are assigned.","expl":"A function assigns exactly one output to each input. Different inputs may share the same output.","fb":["","A function may send several different inputs to the same output.","A repeated input-output pair is harmless; conflicting outputs for one input are not.","Repeated outputs are allowed for different inputs."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-piece","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Compare −3 with the three displayed interval conditions.","expl":"Since -3 is less than -2, use the left-region rule x + 3.","fb":["Exactly the condition containing the input selects the rule.","","The middle interval starts at negative two, so it does not contain negative three.","The right interval contains inputs greater than two only."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-pc21-representations","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (PC21)","unit":"Functions (PC21)","q":"","o":[],"a":3,"hint":"A table records numerical pairs.","expl":"A table gives numerical input-output pairs.","fb":["A verbal representation describes the rule in words.","An algebraic representation is a formula or equation.","A graphical representation plots coordinates on axes.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-rational-cancel-domain","src":"calc","topic":"Domain & restrictions","unit":"Domain & restrictions","q":"Simplify $\\frac{x^2-9}{x^2+x-6}$ while preserving the original domain.","o":["$\\frac{x+3}{x+2}$","All real inputs are allowed","$\\frac{x-3}{x-2},\\ x\\ne2$","$\\frac{x-3}{x-2},\\ x\\ne-3,2$"],"a":3,"hint":"Factor both polynomials, then record the zeros of the original denominator.","expl":"Factoring gives (x+3)(x−2)(x−3)(x+3)​. Cancel x+3, but retain x=−3,2.","fb":["The numerator and denominator were factored incorrectly.","The original denominator has two real zeros, so its domain is not all real numbers.","The removed factor still excludes negative three.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-rational-root-domain","src":"calc","topic":"Domain & restrictions","unit":"Domain & restrictions","q":"After simplifying $\\frac{x^{3/2}+2x^{1/2}}{x^{1/2}}$ to $x+2$, which real inputs remain allowed?","o":["All real inputs","$x\\ge0$","$x\\ne0$","$x>0$"],"a":3,"hint":"The square root must be real and the original denominator must be nonzero.","expl":"The square root requires x≥0, while the denominator excludes zero; together, x>0.","fb":["Simplification cannot remove the original domain restrictions.","Zero makes the original denominator zero.","This includes negative inputs where the square root is not real.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-restriction","src":"calc","topic":"Domain & restrictions","unit":"Domain & restrictions","q":"What must you do before cancelling a common factor?","o":["Cross multiply","Change division to multiplication","State restrictions from the original denominator","Expand everything"],"a":2,"hint":"Look at where the original denominator becomes zero, before simplifying.","expl":"Restrictions come from the original denominator, even when a factor later cancels.","fb":["Cross multiplication is not the first step in simplifying one rational expression.","There may be no division operation between two fractions; inspect the original denominator first.","","Expansion hides factors and does not replace checking the original domain."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-square-sum-check","src":"calc","topic":"Completing the square","unit":"Completing the square","q":"Find only the middle term. $$(2x+3y)^2$$","o":["$12xy$","$6xy$","$9xy$"],"a":0,"hint":"Identify A and B, then calculate twice their product.","expl":"The middle term is 2AB.","fb":["","This is AB; the middle term is twice that product.","Nine comes from squaring three, not calculating twice the product."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-v2-absolute","src":"calc","topic":"Interval & set notation","unit":"Interval & set notation","q":"Given $a<b$, simplify. $$a+b+|a-b|$$","o":["$2a$","$2b$","$a+b$","$b-a$"],"a":1,"hint":"Since a is smaller than b, decide the sign of a minus b before removing the bars.","expl":"Since a<b, ∣a−b∣=b−a. Therefore a+b+b−a=2b.","fb":["This would use ∣a−b∣=a−b, but the inside is negative here.","","The absolute-value term must still be added.","This is only the absolute-value term; add the original a+b too."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-v2-builder","src":"calc","topic":"Interval & set notation","unit":"Interval & set notation","q":"Which roster form matches the set? $$B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid -2<x\\le2\\}$$","o":["$\\{-1,0,1,2\\}$","$\\{-1,0,1\\}$","$\\{0,1,2\\}$","$\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}$"],"a":0,"hint":"Check each endpoint separately and remember that only integers are allowed.","expl":"The strict inequality excludes −2 while x≤2 includes 2.","fb":["","Equality at the upper boundary includes two.","Negative one also satisfies both inequalities.","The strict lower inequality excludes negative two."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-v2-intersection","src":"calc","topic":"Interval & set notation","unit":"Interval & set notation","q":"Find the intersection. $$A=\\{1,3,5,7\\},\\quad B=\\{3,4,5,8\\}$$","o":["$\\{1,7\\}$","$\\varnothing$","$\\{3,5\\}$","$\\{1,3,4,5,7,8\\}$"],"a":2,"hint":"Keep an element only if you can find it in each set.","expl":"Intersection keeps only the elements occurring in both sets.","fb":["One and seven are in A but not in B.","The sets do share elements: inspect three and five.","","This is the union: it includes elements found in only one set."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-v2-interval","src":"calc","topic":"Interval & set notation","unit":"Interval & set notation","q":"Convert the inequality to interval notation. $$-2<x\\le1$$","o":["$(-2,1)$","$[-2,1]$","$(-2,1]$","$[-2,1)$"],"a":2,"hint":"Translate a strict sign into an open endpoint, and a sign with equality into a closed endpoint.","expl":"Strict inequality gives an open endpoint at −2; equality includes 1.","fb":["The right endpoint must be included because equality is allowed.","A bracket at negative two incorrectly includes that excluded endpoint.","","Both endpoint choices are reversed."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-v2-type","src":"calc","topic":"Interval & set notation","unit":"Interval & set notation","q":"Which number is irrational?","o":["$0.125$","$\\sqrt{11}$","$\\frac37$","$2.\\overline6$"],"a":1,"hint":"A square root of a positive integer is rational only when that integer is a perfect square.","expl":"11​ is nonterminating and nonrepeating.","fb":["A terminating decimal is rational: 0.125=1/8.","","A ratio of integers with nonzero denominator is rational.","A repeating decimal is rational, even when it never terminates."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:guided-value","src":"calc","topic":"Limits: direct substitution","unit":"Limits: direct substitution","q":"On the hole graph, which statement is correct?","o":["$f(2)=4$ and the limit is $1$","$f(2)=1$ and the limit is $4$","Both equal $4$","Both are undefined"],"a":1,"hint":"Read two things separately: the filled point for f(2), and both nearby branches for the limit.","expl":"The filled point gives f(2)=1; the nearby curve approaches the open point at height 4.","fb":["The roles are reversed. Use the filled point for the value and trace both branches for the limit.","","The open point at height 4 is not the function value. Find the filled point with input 2.","A filled point defines f(2), and the nearby branches agree. Read their two heights separately."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:implicitChecks-collect","src":"calc","topic":"Implicit differentiation","unit":"Implicit differentiation","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Treat dy/dx as the common unknown to isolate.","expl":"Arrange first: collect the derivative terms, move the other terms, then factor and isolate.","fb":["Substitution into a differentiated relation can find a slope at one point, but the goal here is a general derivative formula, so retain the variables.","Terms can only be cancelled as valid common factors, not by erasing every y.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:implicitChecks-product","src":"calc","topic":"Implicit differentiation","unit":"Implicit differentiation","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Both x and y(x) are changing factors.","expl":"Product Rule gives dxd​(xy)=(1)y+xdxdy​=y+xdxdy​.","fb":["You retained only the term differentiating y; differentiating x contributes y.","","The derivative of a product is not the product of the derivatives."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:implicitChecks-slope","src":"calc","topic":"Implicit differentiation","unit":"Implicit differentiation","q":"For $x^2+y^2=25$, what is the tangent slope at $(3,4)$?","o":["$-\\frac43$","$\\frac34$","$-\\frac34$","Undefined"],"a":2,"hint":"Differentiate first to get 2x+2ydy/dx=0, then use the point.","expl":"The point lies on the circle, and dy/dx=−x/y=−3/4 there.","fb":["The derivative is −x/y, not −y/x.","Moving the 2x term to the other side introduces a minus sign.","","Here y=4 is nonzero, so the slope is finite."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:implicitChecks-trig","src":"calc","topic":"Implicit differentiation","unit":"Implicit differentiation","q":"Differentiate $\\sin y$ with respect to $x$, where $y=y(x)$.","o":["$\\cos y\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}$","$\\cos y$","$-\\sin y\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}$","$\\cos x\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}$"],"a":0,"hint":"Differentiate sine while retaining its input, then differentiate that input.","expl":"d(siny)/dx=cosydy/dx by the Chain Rule.","fb":["","The inner derivative of y(x) is missing.","The derivative of sine is cosine, not negative sine.","The input remains y, not x."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:implicitChecks-y","src":"calc","topic":"Implicit differentiation","unit":"Implicit differentiation","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"The input of the fourth power is itself the function y(x).","expl":"Because y depends on x, use the Chain Rule: differentiate the power of y, then multiply by dy/dx.","fb":["This differentiates with respect to y, not with respect to x.","","The original power is a power of y, not a power of x."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-composite","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"If $g(x)\\to9$ as $x\\to2$, which fact justifies $\\sqrt{g(x)}\\to3$?","o":["The square root is continuous at $9$","$g(2)$ must equal $9$","Every outer function preserves every limit","The input target is positive"],"a":0,"hint":"The condition concerns the outer function at the inner limiting value.","expl":"The square-root function is continuous at 9. Since the inner values approach positive 9, the composition is defined nearby and its limit is 9​=3.","fb":["","The nearby limit of g need not equal its value at the target, or even require that value to exist.","A discontinuous outer function can fail to preserve the inner limit. Continuity is the sufficient condition here.","The relevant outer input is the inner limit 9, not the original target 2."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-conjugate","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to4}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-3}{x-4}$, which conjugate should be used?","o":["$\\sqrt{x+5}-3$","$\\sqrt{x+5}+3$","$x+4$","$\\sqrt{x-5}+3$"],"a":1,"hint":"Keep both terms unchanged and reverse only the sign between them.","expl":"Use x+5​+3 so the numerator becomes (x+5)−9=x−4, which cancels the denominator.","fb":["This is the original expression. The conjugate reverses the sign between its two terms.","","The conjugate must retain the radical term and the constant 3.","Do not change the expression inside the square root; change only the sign before 3."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-diagnose","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"Direct substitution in a rational expression gives $0/0$. What does this tell you?","o":["The limit is zero","The limit is one","Rewrite before deciding","The limit must not exist"],"a":2,"hint":"Distinguish an indeterminate form from a value or a conclusion.","expl":"0/0 does not determine the limit. Rewrite for nearby inputs, then evaluate the simpler expression.","fb":["A zero numerator alone is not enough when the denominator is also zero.","Zero divided by zero is not one. Cancellation must be justified by common factors for nearby inputs.","","A removable hole can have a finite limit. The form alone does not imply DNE."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-difference","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For fixed $x$, simplify $\\displaystyle\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}$ when $h\\ne0$.","o":["$2x+h$","$2x$","$h$","$2xh+h^2$"],"a":0,"hint":"Expand the square, subtract x2, then divide every remaining term by h.","expl":"The numerator is 2xh+h2=h(2x+h), so the quotient is 2x+h. Only after taking h→0 does it become 2x.","fb":["","2x is the limiting value, not the exact simplified expression for nonzero h.","The cross term 2xh must be included when expanding (x+h)2.","This is the expanded numerator; it still needs to be divided by h."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-factoring","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"What is the first useful rewriting step for $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to3}\\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}$?","o":["Conclude the limit is $0$","Factor $x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)$","Use the conjugate","Cancel the $x$ terms without factoring"],"a":1,"hint":"Recognize x2−9 as a difference of squares.","expl":"Direct substitution gives 0/0. Factor and cancel x−3 for nearby values, then substitute into x+3.","fb":["0/0 is indeterminate, not zero. Expose the common factor first.","","There is no radical difference here. The difference-of-squares factorization directly exposes the common factor.","Cancellation applies to factors of a product, not separate terms in sums or differences."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-floor","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-2}\\lfloor x+3\\rfloor$, what happens on the two sides?","o":["Both approach $1$","Left approaches $0$, right approaches $1$","Left approaches $-1$, right approaches $0$","Both approach $0$"],"a":1,"hint":"Just below −2, the inside is just below 1; just above −2, it is just above 1.","expl":"The floor is 0 on the left and 1 on the right. The unequal one-sided limits make the two-sided limit DNE.","fb":["The value at the target is 1, but nearby inputs below it have floor 0.","","Apply the shift +3 before taking the floor; the relevant integer is 1, not 0.","Inputs on the right have 1<x+3<2, so their floor is 1."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-fractional-substitution","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^{2/3}-1}{x^{1/2}-1}$, which substitution converts both powers to integer powers?","o":["$x=u^2$","$x=u^3$","$x=u^5$","$x=u^6$"],"a":3,"hint":"Use a common multiple of the denominators 3 and 2.","expl":"With x=u6 and u→1, x2/3=u4 and x1/2=u3. Factoring the resulting quotient gives the limit 4/3.","fb":["This makes the square root u, but the cube-root power becomes u4/3.","This makes x2/3=u2, but the square root becomes u3/2.","Five is not divisible by either 2 or 3, so fractional powers remain.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-fractions","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2}\\frac{\\frac1x-\\frac12}{x-2}$, which simplified expression agrees for nearby $x\\ne2$?","o":["$\\frac1{2x}$","$-\\frac1{2x}$","$-\\frac1x$","$\\frac1{x-2}$"],"a":1,"hint":"Combine the numerator as (2−x)/(2x) and use 2−x=−(x−2).","expl":"The numerator is −(x−2)/(2x). Dividing by x−2 leaves −1/(2x), whose limit is −1/4.","fb":["The negative sign comes from 2−x=−(x−2); it cannot be dropped.","","The common denominator of 1/x and 1/2 is 2x, so the factor 2 remains.","Combining the small fractions exposes a cancellable factor; the outer denominator does not remain alone."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-identity","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1-\\cos(2x)}{x^2}$ in radians.","o":["$0$","$1$","$4$","$2$"],"a":3,"hint":"Use 1−cos(2x)=2sin2x before applying a special limit.","expl":"The quotient becomes 2(sinx/x)2, so its limit is 2(1)2=2.","fb":["The denominator is squared, so the special limit (1−cosu)/u→0 cannot be used without further rewriting.","The identity leaves a factor 2 outside the squared sine-over-angle ratio.","Using the identity gives 2sin2x, not 4sin2x.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-oscillation","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"Why does $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\sin(1/x)$ fail to exist?","o":["Its outputs grow without bound","The sine function is undefined near zero","Its outputs keep oscillating without approaching one value","Its outputs are always zero"],"a":2,"hint":"Distinguish an unbounded inside expression from the bounded sine output.","expl":"The outputs stay between −1 and 1, but continue attaining values near both extremes arbitrarily close to zero. They do not settle to one limit.","fb":["The input 1/x is unbounded, but the sine output is always between −1 and 1.","For every x=0, sin(1/x) is defined. Undefined at the target alone does not decide a limit.","","Some inputs give zero, but other inputs arbitrarily close to zero give 1 or −1."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-piecewise","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For $f(x)=\\begin{cases}x+1,&x<2\\\\5-x,&x\\ge2\\end{cases}$, which pair establishes the limit at $2$?","o":["Left $3$, right $2$","Left $2$, right $3$","Left $3$, right $3$","Left $3$, right $5$"],"a":2,"hint":"Use x+1 on the left and 5−x on the right; let x approach 2 in each.","expl":"The left-hand limit is 2+1=3 and the right-hand limit is 5−2=3. Their equality establishes the two-sided limit 3.","fb":["Two is the input target, not the right-hand output. Evaluate 5−2.","Two is the input target, not the left-hand output. Evaluate 2+1.","","The right expression is 5−x, not the constant 5."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-squeeze","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"If $-x^2\\le f(x)\\le x^2$ near $x=0$, what is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to0}f(x)$?","o":["$-1$","$0$","$1$","Cannot be determined"],"a":1,"hint":"Evaluate the limits of the two bounding functions, not just their signs.","expl":"Both outer functions approach 0. The Squeeze Theorem forces f(x) to approach 0 as well.","fb":["The lower bound is −x2, whose limit is 0, not −1.","","The upper bound is x2, whose limit is 0, not 1.","The bounds are sufficient because their limits are equal. No explicit formula for f is needed."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-substitution","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -2}(3x^2-4x+1)$.","o":["$5$","$13$","$21$","DNE"],"a":2,"hint":"Square −2 first, then handle the sign in −4(−2).","expl":"Polynomials are continuous. Substitute x=−2: 3(−2)2−4(−2)+1=12+8+1=21.","fb":["−4(−2)=+8, not −8. Recalculate with the positive product.","3(−2)2=12 and −4(−2)=8. Include both terms and the constant.","","A polynomial is continuous at −2, so direct substitution gives a finite limit."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-synthetic","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"For $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to3}\\frac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3}$, what quotient remains after exact polynomial division?","o":["$x^2-3x+2$","$x^2+3x-2$","$x^2+3x+2$","$x^2+2$"],"a":2,"hint":"Include the missing x2 coefficient: divide coefficients 1,0,−7,−6 by x−3.","expl":"Synthetic division by 3 gives quotient coefficients 1,3,2 and remainder 0, so the limit is 9+9+2=20.","fb":["The synthetic number is 3, not −3. The coefficient of x in the quotient is positive.","After adding −7+9, the next quotient coefficient is 2, not −2.","","The zero coefficient of x2 in the dividend must still be included; division produces a 3x term."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:l13-guided-trig","src":"calc","topic":"Algebraic limits (L13)","unit":"Algebraic limits (L13)","q":"Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin(5x)}{x}$ in radians.","o":["$0$","$1$","$5$","DNE"],"a":2,"hint":"Match the denominator to the angle 5x, then compensate with a constant.","expl":"Create the standard form: xsin(5x)​=55xsin(5x)​→5(1)=5.","fb":["Both numerator and denominator approach zero; that does not make their ratio tend to zero.","The standard ratio with denominator 5x tends to 1, but the required ratio has an extra factor 5.","","The indeterminate form can be rewritten as a known special limit, so the limit exists."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-guided-intercept","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"To find the $y$-intercept of a graph, which variable do you set to zero?","o":["Set $x=0$","Set $y=0$"],"a":0,"hint":"Points on the vertical axis have zero horizontal coordinate.","expl":"The y-intercept lies on the y-axis, where x=0. Substitute x=0 and solve for y.","fb":["","Setting y=0 finds x-intercepts instead."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-guided-intersection","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"You found $x=-1$ or $x=2$ where $y=x^2-3$ and $y=x-1$ meet. What must you do next?","o":["Use these as y-coordinates","Report only the x-values","Find each y-value using either equation","Set both x-values to zero"],"a":2,"hint":"An intersection is an ordered pair satisfying both equations.","expl":"Substitution gives (−1,−2) and (2,1); each pair satisfies both equations.","fb":["The solved values are horizontal coordinates, not vertical coordinates.","The question asks for points, so include both coordinates.","","Replacing a solution input by zero changes the point."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-guided-model","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"A tank model is $V(t)=120-6t$ litres for $0\\le t\\le20$ minutes. What does the t-intercept represent?","o":["The initial volume is 20 litres","The tank is empty after 20 minutes","The volume increases by 6 litres each minute","The model predicts negative volume after 20 minutes"],"a":1,"hint":"At an intercept on the horizontal time axis, the volume equals zero.","expl":"V(t)=0 gives 120−6t=0, so t=20 minutes. This is when the tank is empty within the stated model.","fb":["The initial volume is V(0)=120 litres.","","The coefficient is negative, so volume decreases.","The stated model is restricted to 0≤t≤20; extrapolating past that range is not justified."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-guided-point","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"Which point lies on $y=x^3-4x$?","o":["$(-1,-3)$","$(-1,3)$","$(1,3)$","$(2,2)$"],"a":1,"hint":"Substitute the x-coordinate and compare the output with the proposed y-coordinate.","expl":"(−1)3−4(−1)=−1+4=3, so (−1,3) satisfies the equation.","fb":["Keep the subtraction sign: subtracting 4(−1) adds 4.","","At x=1, the output is 1−4=−3, not 3.","At x=2, the output is 8−8=0, not 2."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-guided-symmetry","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"Is $y=x^3-4x$ symmetric about the origin?","o":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"hint":"Replace both x and y by their negatives and simplify.","expl":"−y=(−x)3−4(−x)=−x3+4x, so multiplying by −1 recovers y=x3−4x.","fb":["","Do not replace only x: origin symmetry replaces both coordinates."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-predict-intercepts","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"How many distinct x-intercepts does $y=x^3-4x$ have?","o":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"a":2,"hint":"Factor out x, then use the difference of squares.","expl":"x3−4x=x(x−2)(x+2) has three distinct zeros.","fb":["Do not stop at the factor x; solve the other two factors.","The zero from the factor x also counts.","","A nonzero cubic has at most three distinct real zeros."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p1-predict-intersections","src":"calc","topic":"Lines & linear models (P1)","unit":"Lines & linear models (P1)","q":"Which equation determines the intersection inputs?","o":["$x^2-3=x-1$","$x^2-3=0$","$x-1=0$","$x^2+x-4=0$"],"a":0,"hint":"At a common point, both formulas give the same output.","expl":"Equating the two outputs gives x2−3=x−1.","fb":["","This finds the parabola's x-intercepts, not necessarily intersections with the line.","This finds the line's x-intercept only.","You added the outputs; intersections require equal outputs."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-guided-absolute","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"Using the piecewise rule for $|x-1|$, which expression applies at $x=0$?","o":["$x-1=-1$","$-(x-1)=1$","Both give -1","Neither rule applies"],"a":1,"hint":"Choose the condition first: is 0 less than 1?","expl":"Since 0<1, use −(x−1), giving −(0−1)=1.","fb":["That branch is for x≥1, which does not include zero.","","The valid branch gives positive one, as an absolute value must.","The condition x<1 includes zero."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-guided-composite-domain","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"Let $f(u)=\\sqrt{u}$ and $g(x)=x-1$. What is the domain of $(f\\circ g)(x)$?","o":["All real numbers","$[0,\\infty)$","$[1,\\infty)$","$(-\\infty,1]$"],"a":2,"hint":"The output of the inner function must be allowed as the input of the outer function.","expl":"The composition is x−1​. Require x−1≥0, so x≥1.","fb":["Although g accepts every real input, its output must be nonnegative for f.","The outer function receives x−1, not x.","","Solving x−1≥0 gives x≥1, not the opposite inequality."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-guided-domain","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"What is the domain of $g(x)=\\sqrt{6x}$?","o":["$[0,\\infty)$","$(-\\infty,0]$","All real numbers"],"a":0,"hint":"Require the radicand to be nonnegative for a real square root.","expl":"6x≥0 gives x≥0, so the domain is [0,∞).","fb":["","Dividing by positive 6 does not reverse the inequality.","Negative inputs give a negative radicand, outside the real domain."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-guided-evaluate","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"For $f(x)=x^2+2x-3$, what is $f(-1)$?","o":["$-4$","$0$","$-6$","$4$"],"a":0,"hint":"Put parentheses around the negative input before squaring.","expl":"f(−1)=(−1)2+2(−1)−3=1−2−3=−4.","fb":["","The three terms do not cancel: 1−2−3=−4.","Remember (−1)2=1, not −1.","Keep the negative signs in the linear and constant terms."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-guided-evenodd","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"Is $f(x)=x^3-4x$ even, odd, or neither?","o":["Even","Odd","Neither"],"a":1,"hint":"Compute f(−x) and compare it algebraically with f(x) and −f(x).","expl":"f(−x)=(−x)3−4(−x)=−x3+4x=−(x3−4x)=−f(x) for all real x, so f is odd.","fb":["The calculation gives −f(x), not f(x).","","Both terms change sign, so the result is exactly −f(x)."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-guided-relation","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"Which set of ordered pairs represents a function?","o":["$\\{(1,2),(1,4),(2,6)\\}$","$\\{(1,2),(2,3),(4,7)\\}$","$\\{(0,1),(0,2),(0,3)\\}$"],"a":1,"hint":"Inspect repeated inputs, not repeated outputs.","expl":"Each input in the second set has exactly one output. The other sets assign more than one output to a single input.","fb":["Input 1 has outputs 2 and 4, so the rule is not a function.","","Input 0 has three different outputs."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-predict-composition","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"Which expression is $(f\\circ g)(x)$?","o":["$x^2-1$","$x^2(x-1)$","$(x-1)^2$","$x^2-x+1$"],"a":2,"hint":"Apply g first, then feed its entire output into f.","expl":"f(g(x))=f(x−1)=(x−1)2.","fb":["This is g(f(x)), the reverse order.","Composition is substitution, not multiplication.","","The outer function squares x−1; substitute before expanding."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:p3-predict-quotient","src":"calc","topic":"Functions (P3)","unit":"Functions (P3)","q":"What is $f(x+\\Delta x)$ for $f(x)=x^2$?","o":["$x^2+(\\Delta x)^2$","$x^2+2x\\Delta x+(\\Delta x)^2$","$2x+\\Delta x$","$x^2+\\Delta x$"],"a":1,"hint":"Square the whole input, using (a+b)2=a2+2ab+b2.","expl":"f(x+Δx)=(x+Δx)2=x2+2xΔx+(Δx)2.","fb":["The middle product term is missing.","","That is the simplified difference quotient, not the substituted output.","The whole input is squared; this is not an additive shift of the output."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:pw-guided-continuity","src":"calc","topic":"Piecewise functions","unit":"Piecewise functions","q":"Before checking the derivatives at a breaking point $x=c$, what should you check first?","o":["Continuity at $x=c$","The second derivative","The $y$-intercept"],"a":0,"hint":"Compare the actual value with both one-sided limits.","expl":"Differentiability requires continuity. If the function is not continuous at c, stop: it is not differentiable there.","fb":["","The first derivative cannot exist at a discontinuity, regardless of the second derivative.","The intercept concerns x=0 and does not test an arbitrary join x=c."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:pw-guided-corner","src":"calc","topic":"Piecewise functions","unit":"Piecewise functions","q":"A continuous graph has finite one-sided derivatives $-2$ and $5$ at $c$. What is $f'(c)$?","o":["$3$","$-10$","It does not exist","$3/2$"],"a":2,"hint":"There must be one shared slope; do not combine different slopes arithmetically.","expl":"The finite one-sided derivatives disagree, so the ordinary derivative does not exist at c.","fb":["Adding slopes does not produce a derivative at a corner.","Multiplying slopes does not produce a derivative at a corner.","","Averaging slopes does not repair their disagreement."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:pw-guided-parameters","src":"calc","topic":"Piecewise functions","unit":"Piecewise functions","q":"In Example 4, $p(x)=\\begin{cases}ax+b,&x<1\\\\x^2+1,&x\\ge1\\end{cases}$. Which pair makes $p$ differentiable at $1$?","o":["$a=1,\\ b=1$","$a=0,\\ b=2$","$a=2,\\ b=2$","$a=2,\\ b=0$"],"a":3,"hint":"Use both equations: a+b=2 for continuity and a=2 for matching slopes.","expl":"Matching derivatives gives a=2. Substituting into a+b=2 gives b=0.","fb":["This meets the continuity equation but gives left slope one, not two.","This meets the continuity equation but gives left slope zero, not two.","The slopes agree, but the left limit is four while the right limit is two.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:pw-guided-slopes","src":"calc","topic":"Piecewise functions","unit":"Piecewise functions","q":"At $x=c$, which condition confirms differentiability after continuity is established and both one-sided derivatives are finite?","o":["$f'_{-}(c)=f'_+(c)$","$f(c)=0$","$f'_{-}(c)=-f'_+(c)$"],"a":0,"hint":"One derivative at the join must give the same finite slope from either direction.","expl":"Both finite one-sided derivatives must agree; their common value is the derivative at the join.","fb":["","The height of the join can be any value; differentiability compares rates of change.","Opposite nonzero slopes form a corner, not one shared slope."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:pw-guided-stop","src":"calc","topic":"Piecewise functions","unit":"Piecewise functions","q":"For $r(x)=\\begin{cases}x,&x<0\\\\x+1,&x\\ge0\\end{cases}$, both separate formulas have derivative $1$. Is $r$ differentiable at $0$?","o":["Yes, because both formulas have slope $1$","No, because the pieces do not meet","Yes, because $r(0)$ exists","No, because the slopes of the formulas are unequal"],"a":1,"hint":"Check the left limit and the value at zero before comparing formulas.","expl":"The left limit is 0, but the right limit and r(0) are 1. The jump prevents differentiability even though the separate formulas have equal slopes.","fb":["Equal derivative formulas do not overcome a jump at the join.","","An existing function value does not guarantee continuity.","The separate formula slopes are equal; the failure here is continuity, not unequal formula slopes."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-balloon","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"Air enters a sphere at $5\\,\\mathrm{cm}^3/\\mathrm{min}$. At diameter $20\\,\\mathrm{cm}$, what is $dr/dt$?","o":["$\\frac1{320\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{cm/min}$","$5\\,\\mathrm{cm/min}$","$\\frac1{80\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{cm/min}$","$80\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm/min}$"],"a":2,"hint":"Use r=10 and dV/dt=4πr2dr/dt.","expl":"dr/dt=5/[4π(10)2]=1/(80π)cm/min.","fb":["This uses the diameter as if it were the radius.","A volume rate cannot be copied as a length rate.","","Solving for the rate requires division by 4πr2, not its reciprocal reversal."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-cone","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"For a similar water cone with $r=\\frac5{14}h$, how are the length rates related?","o":["$\\frac{dr}{dt}=\\frac5{14}\\frac{dh}{dt}$","$\\frac{dr}{dt}=\\frac{14}5\\frac{dh}{dt}$","$\\frac{dr}{dt}=\\frac{dh}{dt}$","$\\frac{dr}{dt}=-2$"],"a":0,"hint":"Differentiate the similarity relation with respect to the same time variable.","expl":"The factor 5/14 is constant, so it remains when differentiating both changing lengths.","fb":["","The original relation gives radius from height, not height from radius.","The cone is not a one-to-one radius-height ratio.","A volume rate cannot be used directly as a length rate; differentiate the similarity relation to connect the length rates."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-ladder","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"A fixed ladder satisfies $x^2+y^2=L^2$, with $x,y>0$. If its bottom moves toward the wall, what is the sign of $dy/dt$?","o":["Negative","Zero","Cannot determine","Positive"],"a":3,"hint":"Use dy/dt=−(x/y)dx/dt and the signed horizontal rate.","expl":"Toward the wall means dx/dt<0. Since x/y>0, the top rate dy/dt is positive.","fb":["The minus sign in the rate equation reverses the negative bottom rate.","The bottom is moving and both lengths are positive, so the top rate is not zero.","The given signs and fixed ladder determine the sign.",""],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-shadow","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"If $x$ is the person's distance from the pole and $s$ the shadow length, which rate measures the shadow tip moving away from the pole?","o":["$ds/dt$","$dx/dt+ds/dt$","$dx/dt-ds/dt$","$dx/dt$"],"a":1,"hint":"Write the total distance from the pole to the tip before differentiating.","expl":"The tip is at distance x+s from the pole, so its rate is dx/dt+ds/dt.","fb":["This measures the change in shadow length relative to the person.","","The two distances add along the same direction from the pole.","The tip also moves as the shadow length changes."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-sign","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"","o":[],"a":1,"hint":"Identify the changing quantity and whether it increases or decreases.","expl":"Leaking means volume is decreasing, so its rate is negative.","fb":["The given units are volume per time, not height per time.","","Positive volume rate would mean volume is increasing."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-substitute","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"","o":[],"a":0,"hint":"Instantaneous values describe one instant, not constants for all time.","expl":"Keep the variables alive while differentiating. Substitute the instant values afterward.","fb":["","Substituting changing lengths too early turns them into constants and loses their rates.","A model relating the quantities is needed before inserting the instant data."],"d":""},{"id":"cb:ratesChecks-time","src":"calc","topic":"Related rates","unit":"Related rates","q":"","o":[],"a":2,"hint":"Differentiate the outer power, then multiply by the rate of its inside.","expl":"Every changing quantity is a function of time, so the Chain Rule gives its rate.","fb":["The rate of the changing radius is missing.","Keep the inside r when differentiating its square; it does not become t.",""],"d":""},{"id":"lab:2-4:0","src":"lab","topic":"Chain rule (lesson 2.4)","unit":"Chain rule (lesson 2.4)","q":"Which rule controls this nested function?","o":["Power Rule only","Chain Rule","Product Rule"],"a":1,"hint":"","expl":"One function is inside another.","fb":["Not yet. The inside also depends on x. Choose again and check.","Correct. One function is inside another.","Not yet. There is no product of two separate functions here. Choose again and check."],"d":""},{"id":"lab:2-5:0","src":"lab","topic":"Derivatives of composites (lesson 2.5)","unit":"Derivatives of composites (lesson 2.5)","q":"What happens when differentiating a function of y with respect to x?","o":["Include the chain factor dy/dx","Treat y as constant","Replace y with x"],"a":0,"hint":"","expl":"Correct. Include the chain factor dy/dx","fb":["Correct. Correct. Include the chain factor dy/dx","Not yet. y depends on x: the Chain Rule applies. Choose again and check.","Not yet. y depends on x: the Chain Rule applies. Choose again and check."],"d":""},{"id":"lab:piecewise-differentiability:0","src":"lab","topic":"Piecewise differentiability","unit":"Piecewise differentiability","q":"Step 1 · At the breaking point x=1x=1x=1, what must we check first?","o":["Continuity","Differentiate immediately","Second derivative"],"a":0,"hint":"","expl":"Correct. Differentiability cannot happen without continuity.","fb":["Correct. Correct. Differentiability cannot happen without continuity.","Not yet. Compare the left limit, right limit and defined value at x = 1. Choose again and check.","Not yet. Compare the left limit, right limit and defined value at x = 1. Choose again and check."],"d":""}];
